/** English versions of each lesson's model answer, for interviews held in English. Keyed by lesson slug. */
export const modelAnswersEn: Record<string, string> = {
  "advice-aop":
    "I centralize error handling in one @RestControllerAdvice that returns ProblemDetail: business exceptions map to the right status, MethodArgumentNotValidException returns the list of invalid fields, and a catch-all Exception handler is the last safety net that returns a generic message plus a traceId. I tried removing the advice, and validation errors no longer said which field was wrong while business exceptions turned into 500s. For validation I put @Valid on the request body, use groups when create and update rules differ, and write a custom constraint when I need one; a ConstraintViolationException thrown from the service needs its own handler, otherwise it becomes a 500. I use AOP for cross-cutting work like timing, and I keep in mind that Spring AOP is proxy based, so self-invocation, private or final methods, objects I create with new, and @PostConstruct are never intercepted; the same applies to @Transactional, @Cacheable and @Async. For @Async I configure my own executor with a bounded queue, use a TaskDecorator to carry the MDC over to the new thread, and an AsyncUncaughtExceptionHandler for void methods.",
  "api-reliability":
    "To prevent lost updates from overwrites, I use ETag with If-Match: if the version has changed I return 412, and I can make the precondition mandatory with 428. For webhooks I sign an HMAC-SHA256 over the timestamp plus the body, compare it in constant time, and reject timestamps that are too old to block replays. The sender retries with exponential backoff plus jitter and a dead-letter queue, and the receiver is idempotent by storing the event id. I use OpenAPI as the contract, either generated with springdoc or written first, and I put a spec diff into CI.",
  "auto-config":
    "Spring Boot is an opinionated default configuration layer on top of Spring: auto-configuration, starters, embedded Tomcat, externalized configuration and Actuator. @SpringBootApplication combines @Configuration, @EnableAutoConfiguration and @ComponentScan, so the main class has to sit in the root package. Auto-configuration reads the list of classes in the AutoConfiguration.imports file, and each one has @Conditional; since it runs after my own beans, @ConditionalOnMissingBean backs off. I tried declaring my own ObjectMapper and Boot's bean was not created, and I use --debug to see the Condition Evaluation Report. I group configuration into a @ConfigurationProperties record with @Validated so a bad value fails at startup. The precedence I measured is command-line, then environment variables, then the profile file, then application.properties. Actuator only exposes health by default; I open more on purpose on an internal management port and never expose heapdump or env publicly.",
  "bean-lifecycle":
    "By default a bean is a singleton, one instance per container, but it is not automatically thread-safe: I saw 8 threads doing count++ on a singleton field lose updates, so I write beans stateless and pass request data through parameters. A prototype injected into a singleton is always the same instance because injection happens only once; I use ObjectProvider when I need a fresh instance, and I am careful with scoped proxies because every call gets a different instance. I printed the bean lifecycle: constructor, injection, Aware callbacks, BPP before together with @PostConstruct, afterPropertiesSet, initMethod, and only then BPP after creates the AOP proxy. So inside @PostConstruct, this is the raw object and calling my own @Transactional method has no effect; startup work that needs a transaction I put in an ApplicationReadyEvent listener. A BFPP modifies bean definitions before any bean is created, while a BPP handles each instance and can replace it with a proxy.",
  "collections":
    "Collections has List, Set and Queue, with Map as a separate branch. My default is ArrayList: a dynamic array with O(1) get by index; when it is full it grows by about 1.5 times and copies, so if I know the size I set the capacity up front. LinkedList get is O(n) and it does not use the CPU cache well, so I almost never use it; for a stack or a queue I use ArrayDeque. When I need insertion order I use LinkedHashSet or LinkedHashMap, and LinkedHashMap can also be an LRU cache with accessOrder and removeEldestEntry; when I need sorting I use TreeSet or TreeMap, keeping in mind they detect duplicates with the Comparator, not equals. For top-K I use a PriorityQueue of size k. If I need to remove while iterating I use removeIf or iterator.remove, because modifying directly throws ConcurrentModificationException, and fail-fast is only best-effort. In comparators I use Integer.compare rather than subtraction, and to keep data safe I use List.copyOf instead of unmodifiableList.",
  "collectors":
    "collect gathers the elements of a stream into one result using the ready-made recipes in Collectors. I often use groupingBy with a downstream collector like counting, averagingDouble or mapping, which is like GROUP BY in SQL; groupingBy returns a HashMap by default, so if I want sorted keys I pass TreeMap::new. To get the largest element per group I use maxBy, which returns an Optional, so I wrap it in collectingAndThen to unwrap it. The two traps of toMap are that a duplicate key throws IllegalStateException, so I pass a merge function, and a null value throws NullPointerException because toMap does not accept null values. partitioningBy differs from groupingBy in that it always has both keys, true and false. I use reduce to combine into a single value like a sum; to gather into a List or Map I use collect.",
  "completablefuture":
    "In my project there is a summary screen that needs data from profile-service and order-service. I create two CompletableFutures with supplyAsync on a dedicated executor with a bounded queue, because without an executor they run on the commonPool shared by the whole JVM, which only has about the number of CPUs minus 1 threads. On the orders call I attach completeOnTimeout and exceptionally so that an error or a slow response returns an empty list, then I use thenCombine on the two results: the response time equals the slowest service, not the sum, and I measured it drop from 822 ms to about 500 ms. I use thenCompose when the next step needs the result of the previous one. Errors in the chain are usually wrapped in a CompletionException, so I check getCause. I still set a read timeout on the HTTP client, because the CompletableFuture timeout does not cancel the actual call.",
  "db-design":
    "I normalize the schema so each fact lives in exactly one place, which avoids inconsistencies on update, and I only denormalize when I have measured that a JOIN is the bottleneck. I put UNIQUE, FOREIGN KEY and CHECK constraints in the database because that is the last line of defense for every write path, even when two requests run at the same time. I group related steps into one transaction so they all succeed or nothing happens. For the connection pool I pick a size close to what the database can actually run in parallel and then measure, because an oversized pool is not faster, it just adds contention. For a read right after a write I read from the primary, because replicas lag.",
  "deadlock":
    "A deadlock is when threads wait on each other in a cycle; it needs all 4 Coffman conditions, and I break circular wait by acquiring locks in a fixed order, for example lowest id first, or by using tryLock with a timeout. When a service hangs with low CPU I take a thread dump with jstack, and the JVM already prints Found one Java-level deadlock; in the database, the DB detects it itself, so I update rows in id order and retry the transaction. ThreadLocal gives each thread its own copy, and Spring uses it for the SecurityContext, transactions and MDC; since thread pools reuse threads I always call remove in finally, and I use a TaskDecorator to copy context to the @Async thread. Virtual threads make I/O waiting cheaper: I measured 10 thousand tasks each waiting 100 ms, a 200-thread pool took 5 seconds and virtual threads took about 170 ms; but they do not help CPU-bound work, on Java 21 I avoid blocking inside synchronized because of pinning, and the DB pool is still the real limit. When running multiple instances I lock at the database with a conditional update or @Version, not with synchronized.",
  "entity-lifecycle":
    "JPA is the specification, Hibernate is the implementation that generates SQL, and Spring Data JPA is the repository layer on top. An entity has four states: transient, managed, detached and removed; only managed entities get dirty checking, and with OSIV turned off an entity becomes detached as soon as the transaction ends. The persistence context is a first-level cache that lives with the transaction: I measured two findById calls in the same transaction, and it was one query and the same instance. On load, Hibernate takes a snapshot; at flush it compares fields and generates an UPDATE, so modifying a managed entity does not need a save call. save calls persist if the entity is new and merge otherwise, and merge returns a different managed copy, so you must use the returned value; with assigned ids, save even does a SELECT first. Flush runs before commit, before a related query, and when you call flush, so catching a constraint error around save usually does not work, you need saveAndFlush.",
  "equals-hashcode":
    "== compares the value held in the variable: for primitives that is the number, for objects it is the reference. equals() compares content the way the class defines it, and if it is not overridden it is just ==. A common trap is the Integer cache: -128 to 127 share the same objects so == returns true, but 128 returns false, so I always compare wrappers like a Long id with equals. HashMap uses hashCode to pick the bucket and then equals to find the exact entry, so two equal objects must have the same hashCode; if you forget hashCode, a HashSet holds duplicates and contains returns false. I use records or let the IDE generate both. String is immutable and literals live in the String pool, but the result of == depends on whether the string was built at compile time or at runtime, so I always compare Strings with equals, and I use StringBuilder when concatenating in a loop.",
  "exception":
    "Throwables split into Error, which are serious JVM problems I do not catch, and Exception. Checked exceptions are Exception minus the RuntimeException branch; the compiler forces you to catch them or declare throws, and they are meant for recoverable external failures like IO. In Spring projects I use unchecked exceptions for business errors, because checked ones make the code verbose, do not work inside lambdas, and @Transactional by default only rolls back on unchecked exceptions: throw a checked one and the transaction still commits. I have a base BusinessException carrying an error code; the service only throws, and the @RestControllerAdvice maps it to an HTTP status and logs it once with the traceId. When I wrap a technical error I always pass the cause. I never return inside finally because it swallows the exception, and I use try-with-resources so an error on close becomes suppressed instead of overwriting the original one.",
  "hashmap":
    "Inside, a HashMap is an array of buckets whose length is always a power of 2. On put, it takes the key's hashCode, mixes the high 16 bits into the low bits, and computes the index with (n - 1) & hash, which is equivalent to modulo but faster. If the bucket is empty it places the node there; if there is already a node it compares the hash and then equals: on a matching key it overwrites and returns the old value, otherwise it appends to the end. Since Java 8, when a bucket goes over 8 nodes and the table has at least 64 slots, the list becomes a red-black tree, so the worst case drops to O(log n). When the size exceeds capacity times 0.75 the table doubles, by default at the 13th element, and each node either stays at the same index or moves to index plus the old capacity. HashMap allows one null key, does not guarantee order, and is not thread-safe, so with multiple threads I use ConcurrentHashMap. I always use immutable keys, and when I know the number of elements I use HashMap.newHashMap to avoid resizing.",
  "index":
    "When an API is slow I run EXPLAIN ANALYZE on close-to-real data. I tried a table with 1 million orders: without an index it was a Seq Scan that discarded almost 1 million rows, about 100 ms; with a B+Tree index it read exactly 13 rows in under 1 ms. A composite index follows the leftmost prefix rule, so I put equality columns first and range or sort columns after; an index on (customer_id, created_at) also returns the 5 latest orders without a sort. The index gets skipped when you wrap the column in a function, cast it, use a leading wildcard, or when the condition matches most of the table, and in that last case a Seq Scan is the right choice. I use a covering index to get an Index Only Scan and avoid select *. Indexes are not free: I measured inserts getting about twice as slow after adding 3 indexes, so I only index frequent queries, I remember to index foreign key columns on PostgreSQL, and I use create index concurrently in production.",
  "ioc":
    "IoC is the principle of handing object creation and dependency wiring over to a container, and DI is the most common way to implement it. The ApplicationContext creates singletons eagerly at startup, so configuration errors show up early. I use constructor injection: fields are final, a test just calls new, and a long constructor is a signal that the class does too much. Since Boot 2.6 circular dependencies are banned by default, and I tried it: field injection also fails at startup, and with allow-circular-references enabled field injection works but constructor injection still fails; the right fix is to extract a third bean or use an event. With several beans of the same type, @Qualifier wins over @Primary, and I often inject a Map<String, PaymentGateway> as a strategy pattern. Library classes I declare with @Bean, and I know that with proxyBeanMethods = false, calling a @Bean method directly creates a new instance, so I take dependencies as parameters instead.",
  "isolation":
    "The isolation level decides which anomalies are prevented: dirty read, non-repeatable read and phantom; PostgreSQL defaults to READ COMMITTED and MySQL InnoDB to REPEATABLE READ. These databases use MVCC: READ COMMITTED takes a snapshot per statement, while PostgreSQL's REPEATABLE READ takes it once, so it blocks phantoms as well; I reproduced both. The bug you actually hit in practice is the lost update: I measured two buyers leaving stock at 9 instead of 8 under READ COMMITTED; I fix it with an atomic UPDATE for a simple decrement, @Version when conflicts are rare, and FOR UPDATE when conflicts are frequent. Write skew, like the on-call doctors example, is not prevented by REPEATABLE READ, it needs SERIALIZABLE or deliberate locking. readOnly = true makes Hibernate skip dirty checking and sets the connection read-only; PostgreSQL blocks writes but H2 does not, so it is not a security mechanism. And I never call external APIs inside a transaction.",
  "java-io":
    "I use try-with-resources to close resources and keep the original exception, with errors from close() attached as suppressed. I always specify UTF-8 explicitly so Vietnamese text does not get garbled. For large files I use Files.lines inside try-with-resources instead of readAllLines to avoid an OutOfMemoryError. With a ByteBuffer I remember to call flip() before reading. For Serialization I declare a serialVersionUID, mark sensitive data transient, and set an ObjectInputFilter when reading external data, but for storage and APIs I prefer JSON.",
  "java-runtime":
    "The JVM loads classes through a tree of ClassLoaders, bootstrap, platform and app, delegating to the parent first, so a class's identity is its name plus its loader: the same name with a different loader is a different class, which causes ClassCastException. The JIT compiles hot methods to machine code, C1 and then C2, so Java is slow at startup and then speeds up, and I measure performance with JMH using warm-up. Locks like ReentrantLock or Semaphore are built on AQS, which is a state variable plus a wait queue. StampedLock offers an optimistic read with no lock that you validate afterwards, which fits read-heavy, write-light workloads, but it is not reentrant.",
  "java-time":
    "I store points in time as Instant in UTC and only convert to a time zone for display, because LocalDateTime has no zone and cannot identify a real instant. I distinguish Duration, which is hours, minutes and seconds, from Period, which is calendar based: on a daylight saving transition a day is only 23 hours long, so for something that repeats every day I use plusDays. I know a local time can be missing or occur twice, and I keep system schedules running in UTC. I never share a SimpleDateFormat because it is not thread-safe; I use the immutable DateTimeFormatter instead. In APIs I pass ISO-8601 with a Z.",
  "java21":
    "My projects run on Java 17 or 21, since Spring Boot 3 requires at least 17. I use records for DTOs and projections: with one line the compiler generates final fields, the constructor, accessors, equals, hashCode and toString, and I can validate in the compact constructor; I do not use records as JPA entities because Hibernate needs a no-arg constructor, mutable fields and proxies. I use text blocks for JSON in tests and long SQL, and switch expressions so I cannot forget a break. On Java 21 I use pattern matching for switch together with a sealed interface to handle the different payment result types; the compiler checks that I have covered every case, so I do not add a default. I also know virtual threads let me handle many I/O-waiting requests without moving to reactive.",
  "jvm":
    "The stack is memory private to each thread, where every method call is a frame holding local variables and references; the heap is shared, holds all objects and is managed by the GC; Metaspace holds class metadata. The GC traces from GC roots like local variables, static fields and live threads; anything unreachable is garbage, even if objects point to each other in a cycle. Since most objects die young, the heap is split into Young and Old: Minor GC cleans Eden very often and cheaply, while Full GC cleans the whole heap and should be rare. I leave the default G1. When production hits an OOM, I check metrics to see whether the heap grew gradually or spiked, grab the heap dump thanks to HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError, and open it in Eclipse MAT to find the biggest objects and who is holding references to them; common causes are an unbounded cache, a ThreadLocal never removed, or a query that loads a whole table. In containers I set MaxRAMPercentage to around 75, because by default the heap is only a quarter of memory.",
  "kafka":
    "Kafka is a distributed log: a topic is split into partitions, each partition has its own order and offsets, and messages are kept according to retention so many groups can read independently. Ordering is only guaranteed within a partition, so I use orderId as the key; I sent 5 interleaved keys to a 4-partition topic and saw per-key order preserved. Each partition is assigned to only one consumer in a group; I ran 6 consumers for 4 partitions and 2 consumers sat idle. I use at-least-once: commit only after processing, and make the consumer idempotent with a processed_event table whose primary key is eventId, in the same transaction; I tried crashing before the commit, 20 messages were redelivered and all 20 were skipped. Newer producers already default to acks=all and idempotence, but I still set them explicitly. Failed messages get a bounded retry and then go to a DLT, and to publish an event together with a DB write I use the transactional outbox.",
  "lld-1":
    "I answer LLD questions in 5 steps: clarify requirements, extract entities and actions, put the parts likely to change behind interfaces, code the main flow, then talk about extensions. For a parking lot, I use an enum for vehicle types, pick the smallest spot that still fits, and pull the fee calculation out as a Strategy so it can change without touching ParkingLot. For a rate limiter, I compare fixed window, which is light but allows double bursts at the boundary, sliding log, which is precise but memory hungry, and token bucket, which balances both. On a single JVM I use synchronized; across multiple servers I count in Redis.",
  "lld-2":
    "For ticket booking, I avoid check-then-act by using an atomic operation like putIfAbsent, and I hold seats for a limited time with a hold that has an expiresAt. With multiple servers I lock in the database or in Redis. For an elevator, I use State for its states and Strategy for scheduling: SCAN goes all the way in one direction and then turns around, so it travels less than FIFO, 13 versus 25 floors in the example, and it does not starve far requests. For Connect Four, I only check for a win around the piece just dropped, in 4 directions, and handle edge cases like a full column or an out-of-range move.",
  "locking":
    "Two people buying the last item is a lost update problem: read, compute in Java, write back, and the later write overwrites the earlier one; I measured 10 threads each subtracting 1 from 100 and ended up with only 99. There are three fixes: optimistic locking with @Version, which updates with where version and throws ObjectOptimisticLockingFailureException on conflict, good when conflicts are rare; pessimistic locking with PESSIMISTIC_WRITE, which on PostgreSQL becomes for no key update, the second caller waits, and you need to lock in a consistent order to avoid deadlocks; and an atomic conditional UPDATE with stock >= q, where I measured 20 threads competing for 10 items and exactly 10 succeeded. For batch inserts I use SEQUENCE instead of IDENTITY because IDENTITY disables JDBC batching, 1000 INSERTs versus 20 batches, and I flush and clear in chunks. Bulk updates bypass the persistence context and @Version, so they can overwrite data. I do not use @Data on entities: StackOverflowError, unintended lazy loading, and a hashCode that changes after persist.",
  "microservices":
    "I split services by business capability, each service owns its own database, and I only split when the team and the infrastructure can handle it, because a shared database or having to deploy together makes it a distributed monolith. When I need the result right away I call synchronously with RestClient with a timeout, retries only for idempotent operations, and a circuit breaker on the caller side; when I only need to announce that something happened I publish an event through the outbox with an idempotent consumer, because a chain of synchronous calls adds up latency and multiplies failure probability. External traffic comes in through an API Gateway for routing, authentication and rate limiting, but each service still validates the JWT itself. There is no shared transaction, so I use a saga where each step has an idempotent compensating action, a PENDING state so nobody reads half-finished data, and no 2PC. When calling on behalf of a user I forward the token; when a service calls on its own behalf it uses client credentials. To make it operable I have a traceId propagated across HTTP and Kafka, contract tests in CI, additive-only API changes, and canary or blue-green deployments for fast rollback.",
  "mvc-flow":
    "A request reaching Tomcat gets a worker thread and passes through the filter chain, like Spring Security and CORS, to the DispatcherServlet. The DispatcherServlet asks the HandlerMapping to find the controller method by URL and HTTP method, calls the interceptors' preHandle, then the HandlerAdapter uses argument resolvers and an HttpMessageConverter to read the JSON body, validate it, and call the controller. The result is written as JSON through a converter, followed by postHandle and afterCompletion. If an exception is thrown, the HandlerExceptionResolver hands it to the @RestControllerAdvice, and postHandle is skipped. I printed the order from a real request: with an invalid body, neither the controller nor an aspect around the controller runs, because validation happens while preparing the arguments. Raw HTTP concerns for every request go in a Filter, anything that needs to know the handler goes in an Interceptor, and business cross-cutting goes in AOP. For create APIs I return 201 with a Location header.",
  "n-plus-one":
    "N+1 is when you load a list of parents with 1 query and then touch a lazy association inside a loop, so each element triggers 1 more query; I reproduced it with 10 orders and got 11 queries. I detect it with the org.hibernate.SQL log, where the sign is the same SELECT repeated, and in tests I assert the query count with Hibernate statistics. There are four fixes I measured: JOIN FETCH and @EntityGraph bring it down to 1 query, a batch fetch size of 50 to 2, and a DTO projection to 1. You have to be careful with JOIN FETCH on a collection plus pagination because Hibernate paginates in memory, which I fix by paging the ids first; and fetching two Lists at once throws MultipleBagFetchException. I make every association LAZY and turn off OSIV so lazy loads do not silently run outside the transaction.",
  "oauth2-oidc":
    "OAuth2 delegates access with an access token that carries scopes, and OIDC adds an ID token to tell you who the user is. I use Authorization Code with PKCE S256 for apps with a user, so someone who intercepts the code cannot exchange it for a token, and Client Credentials for service-to-service calls. When I receive a JWT I check the signature, a pinned algorithm, exp, iss and aud, and I never trust an unverified payload. I rotate refresh tokens and detect reuse to revoke the whole token family, store them in an HttpOnly cookie, and keep access tokens short-lived.",
  "oop":
    "OOP has 4 pillars. Encapsulation means hiding data and only allowing changes through methods that enforce the rules, for example a BankAccount only has deposit/withdraw so the balance can never go negative. Abstraction means exposing only what an object can do, like a PaymentGateway interface that only has charge(). Inheritance is code reuse through an is-a relationship, but I prefer composition because inheritance couples things tightly. Polymorphism means the same call behaves differently depending on the actual object: overriding is resolved at runtime, overloading at compile time. On the design side, I follow Tell, Don't Ask: the rules live inside the object, like account.withdraw, instead of being scattered across services, and I use Strategy instead of subclasses when only part of the behavior changes, so I do not violate Liskov. In my project, NotificationService calls send() on a list of NotificationSender, and adding a Zalo channel just means adding one class.",
  "optional":
    "Optional is a box that either holds a value or is empty. Using it as a return type makes the method signature say clearly that there may be no result and forces the caller to handle it, which avoids NPEs. I often use findById(id).orElseThrow(...) in services and chain map calls to avoid nested null checks. An important trap is that orElse always evaluates its argument even when a value is present, so for an expensive default like a DB call you must use orElseGet. I do not use Optional for fields, parameters or to wrap collections, and I avoid get() without checking first. A parallel stream splits the data and processes it on ForkJoinPool.commonPool(), a pool shared by the whole JVM with about the number of CPUs minus 1 threads. It only pays off with large data, CPU-bound work, an easily splittable source and no shared state; I do not use it for I/O in a web app because it would block the commonPool, for parallel I/O I use CompletableFuture with a dedicated executor. Results must be gathered with collect, because adding to an outside ArrayList is a race condition: I tried it and it was wrong every time.",
  "patterns-behavioral-1":
    "Behavioral patterns are about dividing responsibilities between objects. Strategy encapsulates each way of doing something, often as a lambda, instead of a chain of ifs on a type. Observer lets a publisher notify many subscribers without knowing who they are; I pay attention to unsubscribing and to isolating failures per listener. Command turns an operation into an object, so you can support undo and queueing. Template Method keeps the algorithm skeleton in the parent class with a final method while subclasses write the individual steps, like HttpServlet.service(). State gives each state its own behavior instead of ifs on the state, and is often written as an enum. I tell State and Strategy apart by who decides the transition.",
  "patterns-behavioral-2":
    "Chain of Responsibility passes a request along a chain of handlers, where each handler either stops it or lets it through, like the Servlet filter chain. Iterator lets you traverse without exposing the structure, and I use iterator.remove() to delete while iterating. Mediator moves cross-communication into a single central point, and I keep it limited to coordination. Memento saves state into an immutable object for restoring later, and often goes with Command for undo. Visitor makes it easy to add operations but hard to add new class types, and Java 21 has sealed types combined with switch so the compiler checks every case is covered. Interpreter represents a grammar as a tree, like SpEL, and for a large grammar I use a library.",
  "patterns-creational":
    "Creational patterns are about creating objects. Singleton guarantees one instance: I use an enum or the holder idiom for thread safety, and in Spring I let the container manage the bean instead of writing it myself. Factory Method hides the new call so the caller only knows the interface, and adding a new type is just adding a class. Abstract Factory creates a whole family of matching objects, like a widget set per theme. Builder constructs objects with many parameters and validates in build(). Prototype creates copies, and I pay attention to shallow versus deep copying. I only apply them when there is a real need, and avoid adding a factory for every class.",
  "patterns-structural":
    "Structural patterns are about composing classes and objects. Adapter converts an interface so it fits, for example wrapping an old SDK. Decorator keeps the same interface and adds behavior by wrapping, like Java I/O. Proxy stands in for the real object to add caching, permission checks or transactions: Spring uses it for @Transactional, so calling this.method() inside the same class bypasses the proxy. Facade wraps several steps into one simple method. Composite gives leaves and groups the same interface so you can process a tree. Bridge separates two dimensions of change, so the number of classes is a sum instead of a product. Flyweight shares the immutable part to save memory, like the Integer cache.",
  "propagation":
    "Propagation decides what a transactional method does when a transaction already exists. REQUIRED is the default: it joins the existing transaction, so inner and outer are one physical transaction; if the inner method throws a RuntimeException through its proxy, the transaction is marked rollback-only, and even if the outer method catches the exception, the commit still fails with UnexpectedRollbackException, which I reproduced. REQUIRES_NEW suspends the current transaction and opens a new one on a new connection, so the two are independent, and I use it for audit logs; but one request then holds two connections, I measured that with a pool of 1 connection it times out, so it can easily cause a pool deadlock under high load. NESTED uses a savepoint to roll back part of the work; it works with JDBC, but with the default JpaTransactionManager it throws NestedTransactionNotSupportedException. And propagation only takes effect when the call goes through the proxy, meaning the method is on another bean.",
  "redis-patterns":
    "For a distributed lock I use SET NX PX with a unique token in a single command so the expiry is atomic, and I release it with a Lua script that checks the token so I never delete someone else's lock. I know it is not absolute, so the final operation should be idempotent. For rate limiting I use INCR with EXPIRE for a cheap fixed window, or a Sorted Set for a precise sliding window, wrapped in Lua. MULTI has no rollback, so when I need a condition I use Lua or WATCH. For memory, a cache uses allkeys-lru while data that must be kept uses noeviction. For scaling, replication is asynchronous, Sentinel handles failover, and Cluster splits the keyspace into 16384 slots.",
  "redis-structures":
    "I pick the Redis structure based on the operations I need. A counter uses INCR because it is atomic, whereas GET then SET in the application lets clients overwrite each other. An object uses a Hash so I can update individual fields. A leaderboard uses a Sorted Set because the order is maintained for you. Exact unique counting uses a Set, but for tens of millions I use HyperLogLog with fixed memory and about 1% error. For messaging that must not be lost I use a Stream with a consumer group and XACK, not Pub/Sub, because Pub/Sub does not store messages.",
  "redis":
    "I use cache-aside through @Cacheable for read-heavy, write-light data, caching DTOs rather than entities, with a JSON serializer, and I always set a TTL because I have seen that the default is no expiry. On writes I update the DB first and then delete the cache entry with @CacheEvict, because deleting is idempotent while setting the cache can be applied out of order. @Cacheable is proxy based, so calling this.method() skips the cache, and I measured DB reads going up even though the key was present. Three failure modes: for penetration I cache an empty value with a short TTL or use a Bloom filter; for stampede I do single-flight, and I know sync = true with the default RedisCacheManager does not prevent it, I measured 50 requests still causing 50 loads; for avalanche I add jitter to the TTL. For data that is almost immutable I can use Caffeine, and for data shared across many instances I use Redis.",
  "rest":
    "URLs are nouns naming resources, and the method says the action. GET, PUT and DELETE are idempotent, POST is not, and PATCH depends on the content; PUT replaces the whole resource, PATCH changes part of it. I return statuses with their real meaning: 401 when not authenticated or the token has expired, 403 when permissions are insufficient, 409 on a state conflict, 422 on a business rule violation, and 429 with Retry-After. For a payment POST I require an Idempotency-Key generated by the client and reused on retry, and the server stores the key with a unique constraint together with a hash of the body; I tried 4 calls including one retry, and the payment gateway was only called 2 times. I cap the page size, version with /api/v1, and only add fields, never remove them or change their meaning. Errors come back as ProblemDetail from one RestControllerAdvice, and I always map to DTOs with records instead of returning entities.",
  "sd-concepts":
    "At my level, I focus on the reasons and the trade-offs. To scale horizontally the app has to be stateless: sessions, in-memory caches, @Scheduled jobs and local files all have to move to a shared place. To reduce DB load I use cache-aside with Redis, update the DB and then delete the key on writes, and watch out for stampede, penetration and avalanche. Work that does not need to happen right away I push through a message queue, with idempotent consumers because delivery is at-least-once. When calling other services I always set a timeout, only retry idempotent operations with backoff, and use a circuit breaker on the caller side. There is no shared transaction across services, so I use a saga with compensating actions, and an outbox so the DB write and the event publish never diverge. Finally there are logs, metrics and traces so I know where the system is slow.",
  "sd-foundations":
    "Sync means the caller waits for the result, async means it gets the result later through a queue or a callback; polling is the client asking repeatedly, push is the server sending through SSE or WebSocket. A request goes through DNS, CDN, load balancer, API gateway, application, cache and then the database; a reverse proxy sits in front of servers, a forward proxy sits in front of clients. Scalability is handling growing load, high availability is staying up thanks to redundancy, and resiliency is one failing component not taking down the whole system, thanks to timeouts, retries and circuit breakers. A public subnet accepts connections from the internet, a private subnet holds the app and the database and goes out through NAT; object storage stores files by key, block storage is a disk attached to a machine, and file storage is a shared directory. Compose runs several containers on one machine, Kubernetes manages a whole cluster with self-healing, scaling and rolling updates; when readiness fails a Pod stops receiving traffic, and I investigate a 503 through pods, events, logs, endpoints and the latest rollout. When traffic spikes and the system slows down, I measure first, follow the request path to find the bottlenecked layer, and fix that layer. For distributed transactions I avoid them if I can, otherwise I use a saga, an outbox and idempotent consumers; retries happen at only one layer, on idempotent operations, with backoff and timeouts.",
  "sd-practice":
    "I follow seven steps: clarify requirements, estimate, API, data model, high-level diagram, deep dive, and trade-offs. For a URL shortener, assuming 1 million links a day with 100 times more reads, writes are only about 12 per second and reads about 1,200, and 5 years is about 1 TB, so a single PostgreSQL is enough and the focus is a Redis cache for redirects; codes come from a sequence plus Base62, and 6 characters is more than enough; I use 302 if click tracking is needed. For checkout, throughput is not large, the hard part is correctness: I prevent overselling with an UPDATE conditioned on stock being greater than or equal to the quantity, prevent duplicate orders with an Idempotency-Key under a unique constraint, never call payment inside a transaction, and handle duplicate webhooks with a unique provider_txn_id plus conditional state transitions. Post-payment events go through an outbox.",
  "security":
    "Spring Security is a filter chain in front of the DispatcherServlet; with JWT, BearerTokenAuthenticationFilter verifies the signature and exp and sets the Authentication, AuthorizationFilter checks permissions, and ExceptionTranslationFilter turns failures into 401 when not authenticated or 403 when permissions are missing. I configure it with a SecurityFilterChain bean, stateless, CSRF disabled for a Bearer API, and I remember to configure the converter so the roles claim becomes ROLE_. A JWT is only signed, not encrypted: I decoded the payload without any key, and changing USER to ADMIN got a 401 invalid signature, so I never put sensitive data in the token. For microservices I use RS256 with JWKS; access tokens are short-lived, and refresh tokens are stored server-side with rotation so they can be revoked. I hash passwords with BCrypt and compare with matches; I measured strength 10 at about 88 ms per hash. CORS is the browser allowing you to read a cross-origin response, CSRF is an attack that relies on cookies; and finally I check ownership on the server and always bind SQL parameters.",
  "solid":
    "SOLID is 5 object-oriented design principles. SRP: a class has only one reason to change, meaning one party asking for changes, for example not putting pricing, sending email and saving to the DB in one class. OCP: add features with new classes instead of editing running code, often using Strategy instead of an if/else chain, but I wait until the third case before extracting. LSP: a subclass must be substitutable for its parent, so an override that throws UnsupportedOperationException is a red flag. ISP: split interfaces into small ones by what each client needs, so nobody is forced to depend on methods they do not use. DIP: a service depends on an interface received through the constructor, so swapping the database or writing unit tests does not touch the service; DIP is the principle, DI is the technique, and IoC is Spring owning object creation. I treat these as experience rather than laws, and I avoid over-engineering like an interface for every class.",
  "sql":
    "INNER JOIN keeps only matching rows, LEFT JOIN keeps every row from the left side; I put conditions on the right table inside ON, and count with count(column) so NULL rows are not counted. WHERE filters rows before GROUP BY, HAVING filters groups after. For top N per group I use dense_rank or row_number in a subquery. To find records that have no match I use NOT EXISTS, because I tried NOT IN with a single NULL value and it returned 0 rows. For paging large tables I use keyset pagination: I measured offset 900 thousand at about 170 ms versus under 0.1 ms with keyset, with id as a tie-breaker when sorting on a non-unique column, and I use Slice when the total page count is not needed. When I hit a Connection is not available error, I first look for places holding connections too long, like external API calls inside a transaction, N+1, or nested REQUIRES_NEW, and only then recalculate the pool size from the DB core count and the number of instances.",
  "star-story":
    "I prepare about 8 STAR stories from real projects and from my personal order-service project. For each story I only note the key points: one sentence of context, my task, the steps I took along with the options I considered and why I chose one, and a result with measured numbers plus the lesson learned. I keep my project introduction to around two minutes, make clear which part I owned, and plant one technical point I know well so the interviewer follows up on it. One story can serve several questions, for example the N+1 optimization story works for performance, the hardest problem, and taking initiative to improve things. At the end I always have two or three questions back about technical challenges and how the team works.",
  "stream":
    "A functional interface has exactly one abstract method, which is what lets you write a lambda: Function transforms, Predicate tests, Supplier provides, Consumer consumes. A local variable used in a lambda must be effectively final because the lambda copies the value and may run later or on another thread. A stream is a pipeline of a source, lazy intermediate operations and one terminal operation; without a terminal operation nothing runs. When it runs, each element goes through the whole pipeline before the next one starts, so findFirst, anyMatch and limit can stop early; for example filtering even numbers and then findFirst over 1 to 6 only processes up to 2. The exception is sorted: it must receive all the data before it can emit the first element (distinct also has to remember values it has seen, but it still lets elements through one by one). A stream does not modify the source collection and can only be used once. map turns one into one, flatMap turns one into many and then flattens; for numbers I use IntStream to avoid boxing. For logic with many branches or checked exceptions I use a for loop because it is easier to read.",
  "sync":
    "count++ is read, add, write, so several threads can read the same value and lose increments; I tried 8 threads with 5 million increments each and lost more than half. synchronized uses a monitor lock, guaranteeing one thread at a time plus visibility, it is reentrant, and it releases automatically on an exception. volatile only guarantees visibility and prevents reordering, it does not make count++ atomic, so I use it for status flags. AtomicInteger uses atomic CPU instructions with no lock; for a highly contended counter I use LongAdder, which I measured to be much faster. ReentrantLock adds tryLock with a timeout, and ReadWriteLock allows many readers. ConcurrentHashMap in Java 8 locks per bucket, uses CAS when the bucket is empty, reads without locking, and forbids null; but get followed by put is still a race, so I use merge, compute or computeIfAbsent.",
  "testing":
    "I follow the test pyramid: lots of JUnit and Mockito unit tests for business logic, mocking at boundaries like repositories or external API clients; @WebMvcTest with MockMvc for controllers, validation and error format, remembering that my own security configuration is not loaded into the slice automatically, so I @Import it; @DataJpaTest for custom queries, but running on PostgreSQL through Testcontainers, because I have seen a native ON CONFLICT query fail with a syntax error on H2; and a few @SpringBootTest tests with Testcontainers and @ServiceConnection for cross-layer flows like Redis caching or Kafka. I use @MockitoBean instead of the deprecated @MockBean, keep containers static and test configuration consistent to make the most of the context cache. I fix flaky tests at the root: inject a Clock, use Awaitility instead of sleep, and have each test set up its own data.",
  "thread":
    "A process is a running program with its own memory space, and a thread is a line of execution inside a process; threads share the heap but each has its own stack, so local variables are safe while shared fields need synchronization, and singleton beans must be stateless. Runnable returns no result, Callable returns one through a Future; I do not create threads with new Thread, I use an executor, and I remember that submit() swallows exceptions unless you call get(). A thread has 6 states and there is no RUNNING: a thread waiting on I/O is still RUNNABLE, and BLOCKED is only for synchronized. start() creates a new thread, while calling run() just runs on the current thread. Daemon threads are abandoned when the JVM exits. Stopping a thread is cooperative through interrupt: when I catch InterruptedException I call interrupt() again to restore the flag, because shutdownNow() relies on it.",
  "threadpool":
    "ThreadPoolExecutor handles tasks in this order: if there are fewer than core threads it creates a new thread; once core is full it puts the task in the queue; if the queue is full and max is not reached it creates more threads; at max it applies the rejection policy, AbortPolicy by default, which throws RejectedExecutionException. For example with core 2, max 4, queue 10 and 20 long-running tasks submitted, 4 run, 10 wait in the queue and 6 are rejected; I ran it and got exactly that. Because of this order I always use a bounded queue, otherwise max is never used and the queue can grow until OOM, which is why newFixedThreadPool is dangerous, while newCachedThreadPool creates unlimited threads. I estimate pool size as the number of cores times (1 + wait time divided by compute time), then cross-check it against the connection pool and a load test. For shutdown I use shutdown, then awaitTermination, and only then shutdownNow. In Spring I declare a ThreadPoolTaskExecutor with a bounded queueCapacity, because the default is Integer.MAX_VALUE.",
  "tx-pitfalls":
    "I group them into three categories, all derived from the proxy mechanism. Group one is calls that do not go through the proxy: self-invocation through this, private methods, final methods, or objects created with new; I measured that a call through this gives isActualTransactionActive as false, and a final method is even worse because it runs on the proxy itself so injected fields are null; on Spring 6, protected and package-private methods do work. Group two is going through the proxy but the interceptor decides to commit: a checked exception without rollbackFor, where I measured that the row is still saved, or a swallowed exception. Group three is code running on another thread, like @Async, because the transaction is bound to a ThreadLocal. In my project I ran into self-invocation during a file import and fixed it by moving the method to a separate bean.",
  "tx-proxy":
    "The annotation is just metadata. Spring creates an AOP proxy around the bean, CGLIB by default in Spring Boot, and when I printed the class I saw something like OrderService$$SpringCGLIB$$. When a method is called through the proxy, TransactionInterceptor reads propagation, isolation and readOnly, then calls the PlatformTransactionManager, which for JPA is JpaTransactionManager, to get a connection and turn off autocommit. The connection and the EntityManager are bound to a ThreadLocal through TransactionSynchronizationManager, so every repository on the same thread shares the transaction. If the method returns normally it commits, if it throws a RuntimeException or an Error it rolls back, and a checked exception still commits by default. From that you can derive the pitfalls: this.method() does not go through the proxy, private or final methods are not intercepted, and switching to another thread like with @Async loses the transaction. When debugging I turn on DEBUG logging for JpaTransactionManager.",
  "tx-template":
    "TransactionTemplate lets me open a transaction programmatically around any block, using the same transaction manager but without a proxy; I use it for batches with one transaction per row, where I measured that one failing row does not stop the following rows from being saved, and to split short transactions around external API calls. For side effects after commit, like sending email, I use @TransactionalEventListener with AFTER_COMMIT, because a plain @EventListener runs even if the transaction later rolls back. There is one trap: writing to the DB inside AFTER_COMMIT with a normal save is silently lost, I measured 0 rows, so you have to use REQUIRES_NEW. In-memory events are lost if the app crashes, so for messages that must not be lost I use the outbox pattern: write the event to an outbox table in the same transaction, a relay sends it, and the receiver is idempotent. In tests, the test's own transaction hides commit-time behavior and constraint errors that have not been flushed, so when testing transactional behavior I do not put @Transactional on the test.",
  "web-vulns":
    "Most injection vulnerabilities come from letting data be interpreted as commands. I stop SQL injection with prepared statements, and allowlist column names. I stop XSS with context-aware output encoding and a Content-Security-Policy. I stop IDOR with per-object authorization checks on every endpoint, since random ids are no substitute for authorization. For SSRF I use an allowlist, resolve DNS and then block internal IPs, and disable redirects. For path traversal I normalize the path and then check that it startsWith the base directory. For passwords I use a slow salted hash like bcrypt or Argon2 through a PasswordEncoder.",
  "elasticsearch":
    "I use Elasticsearch as a copy for searching, while PostgreSQL stays the source of truth. Its core is the inverted index: an analyzer splits text into tokens, and for Vietnamese I use the standard tokenizer plus lowercase and asciifolding so users who type without accents still get results. I declare the mapping explicitly: the name is text for matching, with a keyword multi-field for sorting and exact filtering; status is keyword. In the query I put what the user typed in must, while status, price and visibility go in filter, because filters are not scored and get cached; for deep pagination I use search_after instead of from and size, because by default it is capped at ten thousand. The number of primary shards is fixed when the index is created because routing is a hash modulo, while replicas can be changed; data becomes visible after a refresh of about one second. For syncing I do not dual write, I use an outbox or Debezium through Kafka, and the indexer uses the database id as _id with an external version so an old event cannot overwrite a newer one. To change a mapping I create a new index, reindex, then switch the alias atomically. For small data I just use Postgres full-text search.",
  "observability":
    "I split observability into three pillars. Metrics tell me whether there is a problem and since when, traces tell me which hop is slow, and logs tell me exactly what happened; the three are linked by the traceId. In Spring Boot 3 I use Actuator with Micrometer and the Prometheus registry: Boot already measures http.server.requests, I add a Counter and a Timer for business events, and tags only use bounded values to avoid a cardinality explosion, so I never tag by userId. For latency I look at p95 and p99 from histograms, never the average. For tracing I use Micrometer Tracing with the OpenTelemetry bridge, the W3C traceparent header, and I export spans over OTLP; with @Async I declare a ContextPropagatingTaskDecorator so the traceId is not lost. For logs I turn on structured logging in ECS format, add fields through the MDC, and never log passwords or tokens. I set alerts on symptoms tied to SLOs and the error budget. When an API is slow at p99, I go through the RED metrics, then USE for things like the connection pool, open the trace of a slow request, read the logs by traceId, fix the root cause and verify it again on that same metric.",
  "webflux":
    "Spring MVC runs thread-per-request, so when a request mostly waits on the database or another API the thread is held, and a pool of 200 threads becomes the ceiling even while the CPU is idle. WebFlux runs on Netty with a few event loop threads, one per core, and no thread is allowed to wait: I/O is non-blocking, and code is written with Mono for zero or one element and Flux for many. Reactor is lazy, so nothing runs until you subscribe; map is for synchronous transformations, flatMap is for when the next step returns a Mono, flatMap does not keep order, and if I need order I use concatMap. Backpressure means the subscriber requests n elements so it does not get flooded. The biggest trap is a blocking call like JDBC, Thread.sleep or block on the event loop: I measured an endpoint that sleeps 100 ms on an 8-core machine, it dropped to just 75 requests per second and slowed down other endpoints too; if I really have to make a blocking call I wrap it in fromCallable with subscribeOn boundedElastic and turn on BlockHound in tests. ThreadLocal and the MDC do not follow the request, so I use the Reactor Context. With Java 21, if the service is CRUD on JPA I choose MVC with virtual threads for simplicity; I keep WebFlux for streaming, gateways, or systems that are reactive end to end.",
  "behavioral":
    "I prepare for the behavioral round with a bank of about seven real stories: a hard technical problem, a disagreement, a mistake I made, a production incident, a time I learned fast, a time I helped a teammate, and a tight deadline. For each story I note the real numbers and the criteria it demonstrates, like ownership, collaboration or learning. When I answer, I open with a one-sentence conclusion, give the context briefly, spend most of the time on what I did and why, then give the result in numbers and what I changed afterwards, all in about two minutes. For a disagreement, I explain how I understood the other side's reasons, brought data, and once the team decided I gave it my full effort. For a mistake, I pick a real mistake of mine, take responsibility and describe a concrete change. At the end I always have two or three questions to ask back that fit the person interviewing me.",
  "docker-k8s":
    "I package Spring Boot with a multi-stage build: the first stage uses the JDK and Maven, copies pom.xml and downloads dependencies first, then copies the code, so a code change does not re-download dependencies. I split the jar with jarmode tools into four layers, dependencies, loader, snapshot and application, so each deploy only changes a few hundred KB. The runtime stage uses an alpine JRE and a non-root user; I measured it on order-service and the image went from 1.24 GB to 431 MB. In a container the JVM reads the cgroup limit, but the default heap is only 25%, so I set MaxRAMPercentage to about 75 and leave the rest for metaspace, thread stacks and direct buffers. I distinguish OutOfMemoryError, which the JVM throws with a stack trace, from OOMKilled with exit code 137, where the kernel kills the process because the whole process went over the limit. On Kubernetes I use a Deployment with maxUnavailable set to 0, liveness points to the liveness health group and never checks the database, readiness points to the readiness health group, and a startup probe covers slow-starting apps. When a Pod shuts down, I rely on graceful shutdown, which is the default since Boot 3.4, plus a preStop sleep of about 10 seconds so the ingress has time to remove the Pod, and I set terminationGracePeriodSeconds larger than those two combined, so deploys no longer cause 503 errors.",
  "distributed-tx":
    "@Transactional only covers one database, so when placing an order touches Order, Inventory and Payment in three services, I have no shared transaction. 2PC works in theory, but it blocks when the coordinator dies, holds locks for a long time, and Kafka or a payment gateway do not take part in XA, so I use a saga. Each step commits locally: create the order as PENDING, reserve stock, then charge the payment, which is the pivot step; if the charge fails I compensate backwards, releasing the stock and cancelling the order with a reason. Steps that cannot be compensated, like sending an email, go after the pivot. Compensations must be idempotent and retried until they succeed, and if one keeps failing I alert so it can be handled manually. For every step that writes to the database and then sends a message, I use a transactional outbox: I write the event to an outbox table in the same transaction, and a relay publishes it to Kafka with orderId as the key to keep ordering. I tried kill -9 on the relay after it sent but before it marked the row, and on restart it sent again with the same message_id, so the consumer uses an inbox table with message_id as the primary key and the second copy is skipped. Since a saga has no isolation, I use the PENDING status as a semantic lock, update stock with increments and decrements, and have a deadline plus a job that scans for stuck sagas.",
  "real-interviews": "When I talk about a project, I split the work into three levels so the listener knows exactly what my part was. First, what I designed and owned, for example how I modelled the document states and the permission checks on the backend. Second, what I built following an existing design, for example API endpoints that follow the team template. Third, what I only read or know at the concept level, for example with Kafka I have only written the producer and I understand the consumer side in theory. For a technology I have not used, I say so directly, then explain how I understand it and how I would learn the rest. I think drawing that boundary clearly is more credible than claiming more than I did.",
  "git-agile": "I picture Git as three places: the working tree, the staging area and the repository, and a branch is just a pointer to a commit. In a team I use short branches and small pull requests for review, update my branch with rebase only while it is mine alone, and merge the PR by squash or merge commit following the team convention. With a conflict I read both sides, understand the intent, then resolve it, run the tests and commit. For undo, restore if I have not committed, revert if it is pushed so history is not rewritten, reset only locally, and reflog can rescue a lost commit. I never rebase or force push a shared branch. If develop has many features but only mine should go live, I cut a release branch from main and cherry-pick exactly my commits, or revert the parts I do not want, or better, ship the code behind a feature flag. Releasing every feature together is a waterfall habit, while Agile releases small pieces as soon as each is done and tested.",
  "maven-basics": "I understand Maven as a build and dependency management tool based on conventions: everything is declared in pom.xml, and each artifact has coordinates, groupId, artifactId and version. A Spring Boot project inherits spring-boot-starter-parent, so thanks to the BOM the starters need no version and upgrading Spring Boot is a one-line change. For scopes, a library needed at compile and run time stays compile, a database driver is runtime, test libraries are test, and provided means the runtime supplies it. Dependencies come in transitively, so I use dependency:tree to see them, and when two paths pull different versions Maven applies nearest wins, so a directly declared version can override the BOM. The lifecycle runs validate, compile, test, package, verify, install and deploy in order. Package builds the jar in target, install also copies it to the local repository, and spring-boot-maven-plugin repackages it as a fat jar that runs with java -jar. I always use mvnw so the whole team and CI share one Maven version. When a build fails I read the first ERROR line to see which plugin and step broke, and for runtime errors like NoSuchMethodError I check version conflicts with dependency:tree in verbose mode.",
  "approval-workflow": "I split it into document, step, approver and history. Each step has a mode, sequential or parallel, and a needed count of signatures, so both kinds of approval and any mix are just data. The overall document status has only a few values such as DRAFT, WAITING_APPROVAL and APPROVED, and every status change goes through a transition table on the backend. The frontend only shows or hides buttons from allowedActions, while the backend always validates again inside the transaction: the status, whether the signer has a seat at the current step, whether it is their turn, and whether they already signed. So that two people signing at the same time cannot produce a wrong result, I use optimistic locking with a version column: the loser rolls back and gets a 409, and signing a second time is a no-op so a double click is safe. For email, I do not send it inside a try/catch because that is not asynchronous. I write an outbox row in the same transaction as the signature, then a worker sends it with retries, so no mail is lost and no mail is sent for a signature that was rolled back.",
};
